module nupp.compiler.version

--[[
What this compiler calls itself.

A release is a tag, and the tag is built from the commit that set the string
below, so the two only agree because a release commit changes this file. Release
CI checks that they do: the archived binary is run and its version compared to
the tag before anything is published, which is why the number can be trusted
rather than merely believed.

Between releases the string carries a `-dev` suffix. A checkout is not the
release it came after and is not yet the one it is heading for, and saying so is
worth more than a number that looks official in a bug report.

Nothing here loads anything, so `nupp --version` answers without the compiler
behind it -- which is also what makes it usable to check that an install works.
]]

local version = {}

--- The release this source is, without a leading "v"; the tag adds one.
version.VERSION = "0.0.5-dev"

--- The one line `nupp --version` prints. A single stable line, because this is what
--- an install script and a bug report both read.
function version.line(): string
    return "nupp " .. version.VERSION
end

--- The interpreter underneath, which is LuaJIT for a release binary and plain Lua for
--- the portable compiler. Only `--json` reports it, being the part of "which nupp is
--- this" that a person answering a bug report wants and a packager does not.
function version.runtime(): string
    return jit and tostring(jit.version) or _VERSION
end

export = version
